2051: re-arm the vsync tick from an absolute deadline (sweetfb_tickfix=1, default off). UNTESTED ON A PHONE, never compiled in a kernel.
What: vsyncFired runs the copy and the flip, then arms the next tick a full period later, so every flip costs an extra tick.
ast19/21/22 fit tick 17.6 ms + 17 ms per flip, a ceiling of about 29 flips/s while swaps reach 35 to 57/s and are dropped.
Apply: git apply this file against research/xnu-local/dxb/xnu's state of SweetFramebuffer.cpp and SweetFbScale.h, then rebuild the kernel. Host check: plans/speed/test/tick-check.c.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFbScale.h b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFbScale.h
index 5ff0e96..129ea4c 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFbScale.h
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFbScale.h
@@ -13,6 +13,21 @@
 #define SFS_NEON 1
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * 2051: the next tick's deadline and the wait to arm for it, in us. A tick that ran late restarts the schedule min_wait from
+ * now, so a long copy or a stall gives no burst of catch-up ticks.
+ */
+static inline uint64_t
+sfs_tick_next(uint64_t deadline, uint32_t period, uint64_t now, uint32_t min_wait, uint32_t *wait)
+{
+	deadline += period;
+	if (deadline < now + min_wait) {
+		deadline = now + min_wait;
+	}
+	*wait = (uint32_t)(deadline - now);
+	return deadline;
+}
+
 struct sfs_job {
 	const uint8_t *base;    /* surface row 0 */
 	uint8_t *draw;          /* panel row 0 */
